\begin{abstract}
Wireless learning experiments are written twice: once against a simulator, and
again---differently---against radios, a testbed's container contract, and
whatever ports that testbed will let through. In this demonstration, we present
UnionLabs, a software-defined radio platform on which an algorithm declares only
\emph{what to transmit} and \emph{what to receive}, and the platform carries
each payload over a real single-carrier/OFDM modem on USRP hardware, over LoRa,
or over no radio at all, with no change to the algorithm. This demonstration
introduces the layer added since our earlier physical-layer work: the wiring of
a multi-node experiment is now a declarative file. One document names the nodes,
the radio each owns down to the connector and RF channel, the port each node
listens on, the address others must dial to reach it through a published
service, and---per link, and per direction---whether that hop is carried over
the air, over TCP/IP, or over LoRa. Every node reads the same file and asks it
one question: which node am I. We demonstrate federated and decentralized
learning running unchanged across a laptop, a lab pair of USRPs, and a shared
Kubernetes-managed testbed, including a chain whose first hop is over the air
and whose second is Ethernet.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Software-defined radio, wireless testbeds, federated learning, decentralized
learning, experiment orchestration, reproducibility.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

%% ===========================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%% ===========================================================================
An experiment that places learning inside the radio loop---federated averaging
over the air \cite{mcmahan2017fedavg}, decentralized consensus over a graph
\cite{lian2017dpsgd}, or over-the-air computation \cite{nazer2007computation}---is
easy to state and expensive to run. The algorithm itself is a page of code. What
surrounds it is not: a modem, an acknowledgement path, which radio sits on which
connector, which node dials which port, and a testbed whose containers cannot
reach one another until somebody publishes a port.

Shared platforms have removed the hardware barrier \cite{powder, colosseum,
aerpaw}, and open physical-layer stacks have removed the waveform barrier
\cite{gnuradio, srslte, oai}. What remains is that an experiment's \emph{wiring}
lives in operators' heads and in shell history: it is retyped on every machine
that takes part, and the retypings disagree---a client dials one port while the
server serves another, or a node is told to transmit on a radio that is
receive-only. The failure appears minutes later, reported by a layer that is not
the one at fault.

In this demonstration, we introduce UnionLabs, a platform that separates
\emph{what an algorithm does} from \emph{how the experiment is wired}, and makes
the second of those a checkable artifact. \emph{The main contribution of this
work is that the medium of every link---over the air, over TCP/IP, or over
LoRa---is a property of the link itself, declared per direction, so that the same
unmodified algorithm runs on a laptop, on a pair of radios, and across a shared
testbed whose nodes cannot address one another directly.} A wiring that cannot
be run is refused when it is read, naming the node at fault, rather than after
minutes of radio time.

%% ===========================================================================
\section{UnionLabs Design}
%% ===========================================================================
As outlined in Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}, UnionLabs is comprised of three layers:
the algorithm contract, the wiring and its transports, and the deployment
substrate.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\scriptsize,
  layer/.style={draw, rounded corners=1.5pt, minimum height=5.4mm,
                text width=72mm, align=center, fill=black!3},
  contract/.style={layer, fill=violet!8, draw=violet!60, thick},
  node distance=1.6mm,
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=1.5pt, minimum height=5mm,
              minimum width=15mm, align=center, fill=black!3},
  rf/.style={-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}, densely dashed, thick},
  ip/.style={-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}, thick},
]
%% (a) the layering
\node[layer] (algo) {\textbf{Algorithm}\ \ \code{produce()} $\cdot$
                     \code{consume(msg)} $\cdot$ \code{on\_result(ack)}};
\node[contract, below=of algo] (api)
  {\textbf{Union API}\ \ payload spec $\cdot$ codec $\cdot$ roles};
\node[layer, below=of api] (tr)
  {\textbf{Transports}\ \ RF round trip $\cdot$ TCP star $\cdot$ mixed chain
   $\cdot$ peer graph};
\node[layer, below=of tr] (phy)
  {\textbf{PHY}\ \ C++ modem (USRP) $\cdot$ LoRa $\cdot$ ideal};

\draw[-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (algo) -- (api);
\draw[-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (api) -- (tr);
\draw[-{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (tr) -- (phy);

%% (b) the wiring the transports are selected by
\node[box, below=6mm of phy.south west, anchor=north west] (n1)
  {n1\\[-2pt]\scriptsize client};
\node[box, right=11mm of n1] (n2) {n2\\[-2pt]\scriptsize relay};
\node[box, right=11mm of n2] (n3) {n3\\[-2pt]\scriptsize server};
\draw[rf] (n1) -- node[above=-0.5mm] {wireless} (n2);
\draw[ip] (n2) -- node[above=-0.5mm] {tcp} (n3);
\node[right=3mm of n3, text width=16mm, align=left] (lab)
  {binds:\\ ack, net};
\node[below=1.2mm of n2, text width=74mm, align=center]
  {\emph{one wiring file: nodes, radios, connectors, ports, and the medium of
   each hop}};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Overview of the UnionLabs platform. An algorithm speaks only the union
API; the wiring file selects the transport for each hop, which in turn selects
the physical layer that carries it.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Algorithm Contract}. An algorithm implements three methods---
\code{produce()}, \code{consume(msg)}, and optionally \code{on\_result(ack)}---and
declares the shape of its payload. It imports nothing from any radio driver.
That single rule is what allows one unmodified file to run over an ideal
channel, over the USRP modem, or over LoRa by changing one flag, and it is
enforced by a self-test that runs every experiment over every radio-free
physical layer. Beneath it is a C++ modem providing synchronization, carrier and
phase correction, equalization, BPSK through 16-QAM with differential variants,
convolutional, LDPC and turbo coding, CRC-16, and stop-and-wait ARQ, over
single-carrier or OFDM waveforms, on USRP N210/B210/X310 radios.

\textbf{Wiring as a File}. A topology is a declarative document naming each
node, its role in the algorithm's own vocabulary, the radio it owns, the ports
it listens on, and the links between nodes. Each node is launched with the same
file and its own identity; everything else---role, peers, ports, device
arguments, antenna connector, subdevice, and gains---follows from it. A node's
plan can be printed without running anything, and a typed flag still wins.

\textbf{Per-Link, Per-Direction Media}. Each link states how it is carried, and
its two directions may differ. This is not a convenience: a rig with one
transmit-capable radio and one receive-only radio has exactly one RF path, so
the reply must return over TCP/IP. A chain may therefore change medium half way
through, with the middle node receiving over the air and forwarding over
Ethernet---which is precisely what a receive-only node in the middle can do.
Wirings that cannot run are refused when read: a wireless direction whose
transmitter has no transmit chain, two nodes on one host claiming one port, a
link to a node that does not exist, or a reply asked to travel over the air.

\textbf{Deployment on Shared Testbeds}. The platform image follows the testbed's
own container contract, staying alive and idle while the platform attaches its
remote desktop. A session hook materializes the shared workspace, publishes what
the node is---its radios, addresses and available physical layers---as a
heartbeated record, and attaches the radio's network into the session's own
namespace. Because a container never receives traffic addressed to its host,
each \emph{listener} must be published, while a node that only transmits
publishes nothing; since publishing renumbers the port, a node declares both
what it binds and what its peers should dial.

