%% ===========================================================================
\section{Demonstration}
%% ===========================================================================
We will demonstrate the platform by running the \emph{same} experiment file
under three different wirings, and letting visitors change the wiring while it
runs. We consider four scenarios, as follows.

\textbf{Radio-Free Federated Learning}. Federated averaging on MNIST, one server
and two clients as three processes on one laptop, wired entirely over TCP/IP.
Accuracy climbs per round with no radio present, establishing that the algorithm
and the wiring are separable.

\textbf{The Same Algorithm Over the Air}. The identical algorithm is rewired so
each client transmits its model over a 915\,MHz link to a receiving radio, which
answers over TCP/IP. Visitors observe acknowledgements, retransmissions, and the
airtime each round costs---the quantity that makes communication a measured
experimental variable rather than a modeled one.

\textbf{A Chain That Changes Medium}. A three-node chain whose first hop is over
the air and whose second is Ethernet. The relay reports each leg it opens, and
visitors can move the second hop back onto the radio by editing one field.

\textbf{Remote Testbed and Deliberate Failure}. The same commands run in a
session on a shared, Kubernetes-managed testbed with a network-attached radio
and published ports, showing that the wiring travels unchanged. Finally, we ask
a receive-only radio to transmit: the run refuses at load and names the node and
link at fault, instead of failing later from the wrong layer.

